(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "2608051200")
  (:adr/title "aiueos as a USB-bootable murakumo.cloud node: prove the boot path, mirror the join decision, and split the workload across two profiles")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-05")
  (:adr/tags (vector "aiueos" "murakumo" "boot" "usb" "kotoba-native" "fleet" "evidence"))
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/superseded-by "")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki"))
  (:adr/authoritative true)
  (:adr/source-format "edn")
  (:adr/context
  "Asked for one deliverable: aiueos compiled with the Kotoba native toolchain, bootable from a USB stick, with the murakumo CLI integrated so the node runs as an \"AI mining server\". The owner clarified the workload: AI mining here means joining murakumo.cloud and serving generation (text, image, video, audio) — not proof-of-work, not the Monero/XMRig fleet the /uriage skill tracks.

Four measured facts decided the shape, and two of them closed off the obvious approaches.

1. The release image was ALREADY a bootable GPT disk (protective MBR, FAT32 ESP, independent FAT16 recovery ESP, aiuefs data partition, plus an El Torito ISO), but every gate attached it as a fixed drive. A physical stick goes through the firmware's USB stack, reports itself removable, and boots the removable-media fallback \\EFI\\BOOT\\BOOTX64.EFI with no NVRAM entry pointing at it. \"Produces a bootable image\" and \"boots from a stick on someone else's machine\" are different claims, and only the second was asked for.

2. The bare-metal profile has NO network stack. PCI enumerates virtio RNG, BLK, INPUT and GPU; there is no NIC driver, no TCP/IP, no sockets. src/aiueos/net.cljc is 41 lines of URL allowlist for host adapters, not a stack. A bare-metal node therefore cannot reach murakumo.cloud, cannot connect to a gateway, and cannot serve inference. No packaging changes this.

3. murakumo's participation logic is ALREADY Kotoba — 33 kotoba/*_core.kotoba pure cores compiled to shipped KIR, including infer_join_core (tiers, relay need, residency clamp, work-kind eligibility) and infer_credits_core (the memory-times-time earning ledger). That is the natural seam with an OS whose decisions are already compiler-emitted Kotoba objects.

4. Those cores do NOT compile to any aiueos native target today. Measured against the current compiler: infer_join_core.kotoba to x86_64-aiueos-user-v1 is rejected with \"typed values currently require the kotoba-script web target, typed Wasm/CLJS target, or the qualified native one-word string/record/variant/option/result slice\". kotoba.kir/only-native-word-typed-features? admits record-get only on a directly-nested record-new of the same schema, and every upstream core projects records arriving as PARAMETERS. Entryless export libraries are separately restricted to the js/wasm/cljs backends. Reusing the shipped cores verbatim on bare metal is compiler work in kotoba-lang/compiler, not packaging work in aiueos.")

  (:adr/decision
  "Split across the two profiles that already exist, and let each claim only what it can demonstrate. Owner chose the staged option (both profiles) when presented with the fork.

BARE-METAL PROFILE — boot authority (implemented).

Boot transport became an explicit axis: smoke-qemu-uefi.sh gained AIUEOS_BOOT_TRANSPORT (disk default | usb), where usb attaches the same image behind qemu-xhci as usb-storage with removable=on.

USB boot is proved by EQUIVALENCE, not by a second green check. smoke-qemu-usb-boot.cljs boots the same image file over both transports and requires: the USB run booted through a USB device path, the disk run did not, the aiueos evidence of both is byte-identical, and both reach the same terminal state. Equality is the load-bearing assertion — a USB run that merely also passed could have diverged silently; identical evidence cannot. Observed boot path PciRoot(0x0)/Pci(0x2,0x0)/USB(0x0,0x0), 33 identical evidence lines.

Two things are excluded from that comparison, each for a stated and separately asserted reason. The firmware banner NAMES the boot device so it must differ — that difference is the positive proof. And each application processor writes a one-character liveness marker straight to the debug port while the bootstrap processor writes its evidence lines, so those characters race: BAAIUEOS_... on one boot and ABAIUEOS_... on the next, of the SAME transport. Comparison therefore starts at each line's own AIUEOS_ marker.

flash-usb.cljs is deny-by-default: it inspects unless --confirm repeats the device path, refuses internal disks / non-removable devices / partitions, asks the OS whether a device is removable rather than pattern-matching its name, requires the image to match its build receipt, and re-reads the written bytes afterwards (cheap flash media can acknowledge a write and store something else). There is deliberately no device auto-detect.

kotoba/murakumo-join-plan.kotoba mirrors murakumo.infer.join in the scalar-i64 slice the object ABI admits, packing the record/option/string shapes upstream uses; os/aiueos/contracts/murakumo-node-v1.edn is the encoding authority. Parity is CHECKED, not asserted: test/murakumo/aiueos_join_plan_parity_test.clj drives it across the full tier x kind x memory x residency matrix — 777 assertions, 0 failures — and the object's own main re-checks five vectors so it fails on the node too, not only in CI.

murakumo gained the operator surface: murakumo.aiueos (image | verify | flash | enroll). enroll deliberately does not reimplement the decision — it calls murakumo.infer.join and prints the wire arguments aiueos's mirror receives for the same node, so the parity the test asserts is the parity the command uses.

HOSTED PROFILE — workload authority (planned, NOT implemented).

Serving generation needs a network stack, TLS and an inference runtime. ADR-0011's Linux-hosted PID-1 profile has the first two for free and is where that workload belongs. Not implemented here and not claimed.")

  (:adr/consequences
  "A USB-booted aiueos node can DECIDE its murakumo participation and cannot yet ACT on it. Named in os/aiueos/contracts/usb-boot-v1.edn and murakumo-node-v1.edn rather than left implicit, because \"AI mining server OS\" otherwise reads as though it were already serving.

USB boot is proved under OVMF in QEMU ONLY. No physical machine has booted this image; real-hardware firmware quirks (USB 2 vs 3 enumeration, per-vendor fallback-path handling) are untested.

The gate does not hardcode a passing exit status. On this host (QEMU 10.0.3) the shared UEFI suite fails at AIUEOS_VIRTIO_INPUT_FAIL queue-or-envelope — a virtio-input device-model difference unrelated to boot transport, reproduced identically on the UNMODIFIED disk path before any change was made. The gate reports AIUEOS_USB_BOOT_EQUIVALENT with the shared status rather than claiming a pass neither transport earned, and AIUEOS_USB_BOOT_OK when the suite passes. This repo has no CI QEMU pin (GitHub Actions was removed by ADR-2607300900), so the environment-specific failure is not otherwise caught.

The join-plan object is a VERIFIED ARTIFACT, not an admitted application. It compiles to x86_64-aiueos-user-v1, but verify-kotoba-user-elf.py pins the runtime-v2 context to the smoke app's shape (fuel 256, capability allow-bitmap byte 60 = cap-call 2,3,4,5, trampoline at 0x1E1020). A pure decision object declares no capabilities and is rejected — measured: \"invalid Kotoba aiueos runtime-v2 context\". Admission is a design choice about what a node may DO with the decision, plus the offline RSA signer (correctly absent from the repo), not a packaging detail.

Exporting it as a KERNEL object would need an entry in kotoba-lang/kotoba-native's elf64.clj kernel-object-entries allow-list — a reviewed ABI change in a third repo. Not taken: murakumo participation is application policy, not kernel mechanism.

The Apple M4 fleet cannot host this profile at all: aiueos bare-metal is x86_64 UEFI and Apple Silicon cannot USB-boot a custom OS. The bootable target is x86_64 PC hardware.

aiueos scripts/tasks.edn, empty since the babashka retirement (ADR-2607173000), now carries the two USB tasks. The os/aiueos/scripts build chain stays sh + Python stdlib: pulling it behind nbb would put Node in the boot-evidence path. New scripts are nbb per the repo-wide no-new-sh rule.")

  (:adr/alternatives
  "Compile murakumo's shipped cores directly for aiueos — rejected today on measurement, not preference (the native typed slice rejects record parameters). This is the right long-term answer and unblocks when the slice widens.

Claim the release image was already USB-bootable and stop — it very likely was; nothing asserted it, and \"very likely\" is not what this stack accepts as evidence.

Put the workload on bare metal by writing a NIC driver and TCP/IP stack — correct eventually, far beyond this change, and would have shipped a half-finished stack instead of a working boot path.

Report the USB gate as passing by pinning the transports' shared failure — rejected: it would convert an environment-specific virtio-input failure into a claim that USB boot passes here, which is true of neither transport.")

  (:adr/evidence
  "aiueos ed6bc80 (main), murakumo 2ec6218 (main), west pins advanced in root 2da35e37 (both verified fast-forward via gh api compare: ahead 3, behind 0).
USB gate: AIUEOS_USB_BOOT_PATH BdsDxe: starting Boot0001 \"UEFI QEMU QEMU USB HARDDRIVE 1-0000:00:02.0-1\" from PciRoot(0x0)/Pci(0x2,0x0)/USB(0x0,0x0); AIUEOS_USB_BOOT_EQUIVALENT removable-media xhci-usb-storage evidence-identical lines=33 status=1.
Parity: Ran 5 tests containing 777 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.
Compiled object executed under nbb via the cljs backend: main => 42, all five vectors matching an independent re-derivation.
Regression: the unmodified disk path produces the same 33 evidence lines and the same status after the transport change as before it.
Flash guards exercised against a real internal disk (dry-run, nothing written): refused with \"/dev/disk0 is an INTERNAL disk\"; receipt digest verified as matching.")

  (:adr/gaps
  "[{:gap :bare-metal-network :state :partial :note \"SUPERSEDED IN PART by ADR-2608052200: the LINK layer exists (virtio-net, frame admission in Kotoba, AIUEOS_VIRTIO_NET_OK). A node still cannot post its join plan -- ARP cache + IPv4, TCP, TLS 1.3 and HTTPS all remain.\"}
    {:gap :real-hardware-usb-boot :state :unverified :note \"proved under OVMF/QEMU only; no physical machine has booted the image\"}
    {:gap :join-object-admission :state :blocked :note \"runtime-v2 context pins the smoke app's capability shape; offline RSA signer absent from repo\"}
    {:gap :upstream-cores-native :state :resolved :note \"CLOSED by ADR-2608052000: the native typed slice now admits record parameters, option/result and keyword at a boundary, and native targets carry entryless libraries. murakumo shipped cores compiling natively went 0/33 to 14/33; the remaining 19 are tracked there, not here.\"}
    {:gap :hosted-profile-workload :state :planned :note \"generation serving belongs to ADR-0011 PID-1 profile; not implemented\"}
    {:gap :shared-uefi-suite-on-qemu-10 :state :corrected :note \"THIS ENTRY WAS TOO BROAD. It said the shared UEFI suite fails on QEMU 10.0.3. Measured 2026-08-05 while building the link layer: the suite PASSES on this same host and QEMU (AIUEOS_UEFI_SMOKE_OK, exit 0) on the default ESP-directory boot path, both with and without a NIC. The AIUEOS_VIRTIO_INPUT_FAIL observed earlier was on the GPT DISK-IMAGE path (AIUEOS_DISK_IMAGE set), which is the only path it was ever measured on. Whether it still fails there is unmeasured -- a re-run was started and abandoned when the host reached load average ~250, where a TCG boot is indistinguishable from a hang. The USB equivalence gate is unaffected either way: it asserts the two transports agree, not that either passes.\"}]")

